sec.p:
To compare the dosimetric impacts of tissue deformation, setup errors, and BH uncertainty on the CTV and OARs in the two patient groups undergoing DIBH treatment. Before the online setup calibration, breath-hold CBCT images of each patient were obtained once per week. Adaptive CT datasets were generated using an offline adaptive velocity module with CBCT-corrected multipass deformable registration algorithms [27]. Anatomical structures (Body, Lung-L, Breast-R, Heart, CTV, and LAD) were propagated from planning to adaptive CTs using validated deformable image registration. The original treatment plan was subsequently recalculated on each adaptive CT using identical beam parameters, generating adaptive dose (Adaptive Dose) distributions for per-fraction. Four adaptive fraction doses were co-registered to the planning CT using validated deformable image registration (DIR) algorithms and subsequently averaged, generating deformation-corrected accumulated dose (Accumulated Dose) distributions for the CTV and OARs across both groups. The adaptive and accumulated dose calculation process is illustrated in Fig. 3. Quantitative dosimetric comparisons were performed for both groups to evaluate the Accumulated Dose distributions versus the planned dose (Plan Dose) distributions across the following parameters: Heart: Dmean, V20, V10, V5; Lung-L: Dmean, V30, V20, V5; LAD: Dmean, V40, V30; Breast-R༚Dmean ; and CTV: V95%, V110%, V107%, D2%, andD98%. To evaluate the stability of the BH treatment guided by both groups for each session, the coefficient of variation (CV) was used to measure the variability of doses received by the CTV and OARs across treatments. A smaller CV indicated less variability and higher treatment stability for the patient.1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
